# Goblin — first port from the WAM cast (~/Projects/2026/horse/models/goblin.wam),
# and the first character authored in .blob that was not the zombie.
#
# WAM builds bodies from `loft`s: a profile swept through rings along a bone
# chain. .blob has no loft, so every ring becomes a `blob` at the same relative
# position. That mapping is closer than it sounds — a ring IS an ellipse on a
# bone, which is what a blob is — but two things do not survive it:
#
#   * WAM's `w`/`d` are FULL width and depth; a blob's `r` is a radius. So
#     r = w/2, and deep = d/w to keep the cross-section's aspect.
#   * WAM rings run over a bone CHAIN with t normalised across the whole chain;
#     .blob prims ride ONE bone. Every `at` below was recomputed by walking the
#     chain length. The torso chain is pelvis(0.14) + spine1(0.10) + chest(0.12)
#     = 0.36, so WAM's t=0.58 lands 0.209 along, i.e. spine1 at 0.69.
#
# Silhouette intent, carried over from the WAM file: stooped and scheming, not
# standing at attention. Spine pitches forward, neck pitches back to bring the
# face level, arms hang long and slightly ahead, legs bow out at the thigh and
# back in at the shin. None of that is animation — it is all rest pose.
model goblin
  height 1.30
  # humanoid | digitigrade. Checked, not decorative — see blob-checks.ts.
  stance humanoid

skeleton
  # ELONGATED, not squat. The first pass put the hip at 0.425 of a 1.15 m body
  # with 0.385 of leg under it, and the result was an egg with stumps: the
  # torso was the whole silhouette and the limbs barely escaped it. Legs are
  # now roughly half the standing height, the torso is longer and much
  # narrower, and every limb segment is a distinct length rather than a blob.
  root pelvis at 0.60 len=0.105

  bone spine1 parent=pelvis dir=up pitch=13 len=0.135
  bone chest  parent=spine1 dir=up pitch=9  len=0.150
  # No-neck syndrome took four changes, and the non-obvious one was the head.
  # facePrims centres the cranium partway along the skull bone, so an oversized
  # headRadius on a short skull bone puts the bottom of that ellipsoid BELOW
  # the neck's top — the head does not sit near the shoulders, it encloses the
  # whole neck, and thinning the column cannot reveal something inside a
  # sphere. Cranium size, skull length, neck length and the chest cap all move
  # together; none of them works alone.
  bone neck   parent=chest  dir=up pitch=-17 len=0.140
  bone skull  parent=neck   dir=up pitch=-5  len=0.235

  mirror
    # WAM's own comment says the shoulder must land INSIDE the chest mass or
    # "the arm floats free". Right for WAM, where the arm is a separate mesh
    # that has to overlap to look attached — and exactly WRONG here, because in
    # an SDF overlapping IS merging and smooth-min swallowed both arms whole.
    # Overshooting is just as wrong: too far out and fusedOf goes POSITIVE, the
    # core-to-core path leaves the flesh and the arm hangs on by nothing. The
    # checks find both edges of a narrow band, and the band MOVES when the
    # torso changes: narrowing the chest to fix the egg silhouette pushed the
    # old shoulder reach outside the flesh and detached both arms outright
    # (fusedOf +0.0076, and validateBody reported the clusters disconnected).
    # Shoulder reach is a function of chest width, not a constant.
    #
    # And "attached" is not the target — DISTINCT is. At side 0.044 + len 0.050
    # the shoulder sat at x 0.094 with a 0.044 nub, against a chest half-width
    # of 0.086: most of the nub was buried and the arms melted into the torso
    # even though every check passed. Fused is a floor, not the goal.
    #
    # Reach alone cannot fix it, though — measured sweep against the ORIGINAL
    # wide torso: 0.046/0.050 left 5 mm of margin, 0.050/0.056 left 0.8 mm,
    # 0.054/0.062 was already detached by fusedOf, and 0.058/0.068 was detached
    # loudly enough that validateBody reported both arms disconnected. There is
    # no reach that both attaches and clears a torso that wide.
    #
    # THE FIX WAS NOT ON THE ARM AT ALL — it was the torso's CROSS-SECTION.
    # Reach, tilt and shoulder radius are all fighting one number: how far out
    # in x the torso's flesh reaches at the height the arm is passing. The arm
    # has to start inside that flesh (or it detaches) and then get outside it
    # (or it melds), so every lever on the arm is trying to win a race the
    # torso sets the length of. Measured on the wide torso, the upper arm's
    # closest approach was 5.4 mm of air at its own midpoint and the forearm's
    # was 18 mm — daylight so thin that at 1.30 m tall neither limb read as
    # separate from any angle, on either the lit or the shadowed side.
    #
    # So the torso gave up width and took it back as DEPTH: every torso ring
    # narrowed in x and grew its `deep` to match, which keeps the creature's
    # bulk (it reads as a pot belly in profile, which is more goblin than the
    # ovoid was) while vacating the exact axis the arms need. That freed the
    # whole budget at once — with the gut at r=0.086 instead of 0.098 a modest
    # tilt of 22 clears it, where 26+ was needed before and read as chicken
    # wings. Measured after: 46 mm at the elbow, 44 mm at the forearm midpoint,
    # 59 mm at the wrist. The clavicle came in to 0.036/0.042 in step, because
    # shoulder reach is a function of chest width and the chest moved.
    #
    # The knock-on was free: the forearm no longer has to tuck in hard to keep
    # the hands beside the hips, so tilt went -9 to -5, and the hands stopped
    # sitting 12 mm INSIDE the thighs (clear(armL,legL) -0.0121 to +0.0227).
    # The hips narrowed with them (side 0.072 to 0.058, tilt 7 to 5), which the
    # elongated silhouette wanted anyway.
    #
    # characters/goblin-blob.test.ts pins all of this. Arm clearance is not
    # something you can eyeball from the numbers in this file — it is an
    # emergent property of the torso, the clavicle and both arm tilts at once,
    # and it has now silently regressed twice.
    bone clavicle parent=chest    side=0.036 dir=side  len=0.042
    bone upperarm parent=clavicle dir=down pitch=-12 tilt=22 len=0.235
    bone forearm  parent=upperarm dir=down pitch=11 tilt=-5 len=0.235
    bone hand     parent=forearm  dir=down             len=0.072
    # Long bowed legs: splay out at the thigh, back in at the shin, so the knee
    # stands proud of the ankle line.
    #
    # PITCH SIGNS ARE FLIPPED FROM THE WAM SOURCE, deliberately. Ported
    # verbatim (thigh pitch=-9, shin pitch=+11) they gave a digitigrade leg —
    # thigh angling BACK and shin forward puts the knee behind the hip-to-ankle
    # line, which reads as a kangaroo hock rather than a knee. In .blob a
    # positive pitch always carries a bone toward +z, so a humanoid knee wants
    # the opposite: thigh FORWARD, shin back, ankle ending up under the hip.
    #
    # This is also the rule the motion work already settled for the zombie —
    # `ik.poleReflect` exists because an unconstrained FABRIK solve folded the
    # knee backwards and kept it, which is the "cow walk" in that code's own
    # comments. A character authored with a backward knee at rest would fight
    # its own IK.
    bone thigh    parent=pelvis   dir=down tilt=5  pitch=9  side=0.058 len=0.290
    bone shin     parent=thigh    dir=down tilt=-7 pitch=-11 len=0.270
    bone foot     parent=shin     dir=fwd              len=0.130
  end

body
  # Torso: long and narrow with the gut low, rather than one wide ovoid.
  #
  # Every ring here is narrow in X and DEEP in Z, and that trade is the reason
  # the arms read — see the shoulder note in the skeleton block. Width in x is
  # the one budget the arms are competing for; depth in z costs them nothing,
  # so the bulk moved there and the creature reads pot-bellied in profile
  # instead of ovoid from the front. Do not "fix" a deep value back toward 1
  # without re-checking goblin-blob.test.ts: the gut at deep=1.38 is carrying
  # mass that used to sit in the r, and shrinking it just makes the goblin
  # thin rather than giving anything back.
  blob torso on pelvis at=0.00 r=0.072 deep=1.18 blend=0.0045
  blob torso on pelvis at=0.90 r=0.086 deep=1.38 blend=0.0050
  blob torso on spine1 at=0.62 r=0.080 deep=1.26 blend=0.0050
  blob torso on chest  at=0.45 r=0.071 deep=1.14 blend=0.0042
  blob torso on chest  at=1.00 r=0.062 deep=1.02 blend=0.0040

  # Neck column — thin, and rising out of a small chest cap.
  bar head on neck from=0.00 to=1.00 r=0.046 deep=0.94 blend=0.0016

  # Ears: ONE tapered prim each, swept out and back to a point.
  #
  # This used to be two blobs apiece, and the comment here said why: "one
  # ellipsoid cannot taper — it is round whatever you scale it to — so each ear
  # is a flattened base plus a much smaller tip pushed further out and up."
  # That is a description of the format's limit, not of an ear. The smooth-min
  # between the two prims was doing the tapering, which meant the taper was a
  # FILLET — convex, bulging out before it came back in — and the tip was still
  # a small sphere. Mickey Mouse, which is what the owner called it.
  #
  # `r2=0.003` is a real point, and `tip=` sweeps it out and up out of a skull
  # bone that runs straight up. `chamfer` creases the ear into the head instead
  # of letting it melt in. The skull's own half-width is headRadius x headWidth
  # = 0.121, so the base at x 0.086 sits INSIDE the cranium (which is what
  # attaches it) and the point stands 52 mm clear.
  blob head on skull at=0.58 r=0.036 r2=0.003 wide=0.80 deep=0.44 blend=0.0020 chamfer offset=(0.086,0.006,-0.006) tip=(0.087,0.104,-0.022) both

  # Nose — A REAL HOOK, ONE PRIMITIVE.
  #
  # What was here: two tapered prims — a bridge running down and out from
  # between the eyes, then a smaller hooked tip turning down off its end. That
  # was itself an upgrade over the ORIGINAL two ellipsoids faked into a hook
  # (which only read from the front), but it was still a chain: the joint
  # between bridge and hook carried the bridge's own round base, and under the
  # smooth-min the two segments read as a sausage with a kink rather than one
  # curved nose.
  #
  # `bend=` makes the whole thing ONE primitive: a capsule swept along a
  # quadratic Bezier from the bridge's start to the hook's point, control
  # point pushed FORWARD of the chord so the curve goes out along the old
  # bridge direction and then turns down — the bend is the goblin, and the
  # round base at the old joint is gone entirely. The radius lerps along the
  # CURVE now, and at the old joint (t ≈ 0.57) it reads 10 mm — the old hook
  # prim's radius, so the silhouette keeps its slenderness through the turn.
  #
  # Aiming the bend: the chord midpoint sits at (0,-0.043,0.116) relative to
  # the anchor; the control point wants to be where the bridge and hook
  # TANGENTS intersect, at (0,-0.041,0.143) — so the displacement from the
  # midpoint is (0, +0.002, +0.027). The apex bulges halfway out, ~13 mm
  # forward of the chord, which is the hook.
  #
  # REACH still rules: the point sits at z=0.162, 44 mm proud of the
  # cranium's 0.118 m semi-depth. SLENDER still rules: r=0.019 at the base
  # (a fat base read as a bulb wearing a spike), r2=0.003 at the point.
  blob head on skull at=0.47 r=0.019 r2=0.003 wide=0.80 blend=0.0016 chamfer offset=(0,-0.010,0.070) tip=(0,-0.066,0.092) bend=(0,0.002,0.027)

  # BIG LIPS — a protruding muzzle, the cartoon-mouse read.
  #
  # The face SHEET already draws a mouth, but a painted mouth is flat: it
  # cannot break the silhouette, so from any angle off dead-front the goblin
  # had a smooth jaw with a dark smear on it. Lips are the one facial feature
  # that has to be geometry, because the whole point of them is that they stick
  # out.
  #
  # Two prims, creased against each other with `chamfer` rather than blended:
  # a fillet between an upper and lower lip fuses them into one sausage, and
  # the SEAM is what makes it read as a mouth. `wide` well above 1 spreads them
  # across the face; `tall` well under 1 keeps each one a flat band rather than
  # a ball.
  #
  # Sitting below the nose (skull at=0.47) and above the jaw, so all three
  # features stack instead of competing for the same flesh.
  # REACH, again: the cranium's semi-depth is 0.118, so lips whose front edge
  # lands short of that are a texture with extra steps. These reach 0.130 —
  # 12 mm proud, and deliberately no more.
  #
  # THE NOSE HAS TO STAY THE FACE'S LEAD. A first pass at r=0.040 / wide=1.70
  # reached 0.142 and spread most of the way across the head, and the goblin
  # turned into a cartoon mouse: the lips became the muzzle and the hooked nose
  # read as part of it. The nose tips out at 0.162, so keeping the lips a clear
  # 30 mm behind it is what preserves the hook as the silhouette's leading
  # edge. Lips, not a snout.
  #
  # AND THEY HAVE TO SIT BELOW THE NOSE, which is not automatic. At skull 0.31
  # the upper lip landed at y=1.194 and the nose tip at y=1.1935 — the same
  # height — so smooth-min fused them into one broad snout with a spike on the
  # front. The owner read it as "a big broad disk intersecting the nose", and
  # that is exactly what two features competing for the same flesh looks like.
  # Dropped to 0.12 — 5.6 cm of face between the nose tip and the upper lip.
  # 0.23 was a first attempt and STILL merged: 2 cm is inside the reach of the
  # nose's own blend plus the lips', so the two masses found each other anyway.
  # Separation has to beat the sum of the blends, not just be nonzero.
  #
  # The jaw grew to make the room (jawDrop 0.072 to 0.104). Lengthening the
  # lower face is the honest fix: cramming three features into a short muzzle
  # is what forced them to overlap, and shrinking the lips instead just makes
  # a goblin with no mouth.
  #
  # Their reach came down with the move. The cranium is an ellipsoid, so its
  # semi-depth SHRINKS as you go lower — 0.108 at this height, not the 0.118 at
  # the nose — and lips sized against the wrong number stick out too far.
  blob head on skull at=0.12 r=0.028 r2=0.021 wide=1.25 tall=0.44 blend=0.0020 chamfer offset=(0,0.006,0.062) tip=(0,-0.006,0.044)
  blob head on skull at=0.12 r=0.024 r2=0.018 wide=1.12 tall=0.42 blend=0.0016 chamfer offset=(0,-0.028,0.056) tip=(0,-0.018,0.038)

  # THE MOUTH LINE — a groove, not a gap between two lumps.
  #
  # The crease between the lips was being faked by creasing the two lip prims
  # against each other with `chamfer`, which only works while both lips exist,
  # both touch, and the camera is near enough to front-on to see the join. A
  # groove cuts a CHANNEL along where this primitive's surface crosses the
  # assembled face, so the line is a line — it survives the lips changing size,
  # and it reads from every angle.
  #
  # Wide and flat (`wide` up, `tall` right down) so its zero-set is a
  # horizontal band across the muzzle. `depth`/`width` are millimetres: the
  # channel is 5 mm across and bites 4 mm in, which on a 1.30 m goblin is a
  # crease rather than a wound.
  groove head on skull at=0.12 r=0.030 wide=1.30 tall=0.09 deep=1.10 depth=0.004 width=0.005 offset=(0,-0.014,0.060)

  # ARMS AND LEGS — ball joints, deliberately.
  #
  # Every segment is a bar with a nub at each joint, and the nubs are meant to
  # read: shoulder, elbow, wrist, hip, knee, ankle. A shaft alone reads as a
  # pipe, and this is the stylisation rather than something to blend away, so
  # the nubs are fatter than the shafts they join and the blends are kept tight
  # enough not to swallow them.
  blob arm on clavicle at=1.00 r=0.054 blend=0.0014 mirror
  bar  arm on upperarm from=0.02 to=0.96 r=0.032 blend=0.0018 mirror
  blob arm on forearm  at=0.00 r=0.038 blend=0.0030 mirror
  bar  arm on forearm  from=0.06 to=0.94 r=0.028 blend=0.0024 mirror
  blob arm on forearm  at=1.00 r=0.030 blend=0.0026 mirror

  # Big grabby hands. Goblins are all nose, ears and hands.
  blob arm on hand at=0.55 r=0.046 deep=0.90 blend=0.0034 mirror

  blob leg on thigh at=0.00 r=0.058 blend=0.0038 mirror
  bar  leg on thigh from=0.08 to=0.92 r=0.044 blend=0.0030 mirror
  blob leg on shin  at=0.00 r=0.050 blend=0.0036 mirror
  bar  leg on shin  from=0.08 to=0.92 r=0.035 blend=0.0028 mirror
  blob leg on shin  at=1.00 r=0.038 blend=0.0030 mirror

  # Feet: an ankle nub, a shaft forward along the foot bone, and a toe nub.
  # Splayed and slightly oversized, which is what stops a long-legged creature
  # reading as stilts.
  bar  leg on foot from=0.10 to=0.80 r=0.032 tall=0.80 blend=0.0028 mirror
  blob leg on foot at=0.92 r=0.038 wide=1.15 tall=0.70 deep=0.95 blend=0.0030 mirror

# The head itself is facePrims(), not geometry authored here — .blob gets the
# zombie's parameterised skull whatever character it is. Pushed toward goblin
# proportions: a bigger cranium relative to a 1.15 m body, a jutting jaw for the
# snout, and a heavier brow for the scowl. The pointed cranium taper and the
# tusks in the WAM file have no analog at all.
face
  headRadius 0.126
  headWidth  0.96
  headHeight 1.02
  headDepth  0.94
  jawWidth   0.70
  jawHeight  0.90
  jawDrop    0.104
  jawJut     0.072
  noseLength 0.006
  noseWidth  0.34
  noseDrop   0.012
  browHeavy  0.014
  browRise   0.038
  headBlend  0.0030

# The goblin's own face, generated rather than inherited.
#
# Before this, every .blob character wore /assets/lab/zombie-face.png, because
# a shared 64x64 greyscale sheet was the only way to have a face at all — which
# is most of why this thing still read as "the zombie with different limbs".
# The sheet is drawn from these numbers instead: features are DARKENINGS of a
# grainy base, because the shader uses the sheet as an albedo multiplier.
#
# Small close-set eyes under a heavy slanted brow, and a wide down-turned mouth
# — a scowl is mostly brow angle and mouth curve, not eye shape.
sheet
  eyeGap     0.44
  eyeSize    0.070
  eyeRise    0.44
  eyeTilt    16
  eyeSquash  1.70
  # 0, not a pupil: a pupil darkens the very pixels the glow keys off, so it
  # trades a pupil for a dimmer eye. See blob-face-sheet.ts.
  eyePupil   0
  browHeavy  0.90
  browAngle  24
  mouthWidth 0.54
  # 0.63, not 0.73. The lips are GEOMETRY now, and the painted mouth has to
  # land on them or the head wears two mouths — a dark smear on the jaw below a
  # protruding pout. 0.63 is where the lip prims sit in face-space: they ride
  # the skull at 0.31, which is 63% down a face spanning y 1.096..1.354.
  mouthRise  0.80
  mouthCurve -0.50
  # A SEAM, not a slab. With no geometry to help, the sheet's mouth had to do
  # all the work and was drawn thick; over a real pout that thickness just
  # muddies the crease between the two lips.
  mouthOpen  0.042
  nostril    0.45
  noseRidge  1.00
  noseWide   0.062
  noseLong   0.235
  jawShade   0.55
  grain      0.10
  seed       5

# The goblin's own flesh, rather than the lab's default preset.
#
# Before this block existed every .blob character wore whichever FLESH_PRESET
# the panel had selected — one global object — so the whole cast rendered as
# the same pink creature in different shapes, which is most of why the first
# goblin read as "the zombie with different limbs" even after the skeleton was
# entirely rebuilt. Colour does more work than proportion here.
#
# These are the field names of FleshMaterial (material.ts), not friendlier
# aliases, for the reason the face block uses FaceParams' own names: an alias
# table is a second list that drifts from the first. Anything not named here
# keeps henenlotter-latex's value — a palette is a partial override, and the
# base is a NAMED preset rather than the panel's current selection so the same
# .blob renders the same in the lab, in a turntable capture and in the game.
#
# Linear RGB. These go through the output sRGB encode on the WebGPU path, which
# lifts the low channels hard — that is why the green is much less saturated
# here than it looks on screen, and why nudging blue up 0.02 does more than it
# reads like it should.
palette
  baseColor     0.34 0.44 0.19
  # Wounds stay red. A green creature bleeding green reads as a plant.
  deepColor     0.46 0.09 0.09
  charColor     0.06 0.07 0.05
  # Clammy rather than latex-wet: a lower, broader highlight than the zombie's.
  specIntensity 0.52
  specRoughness 0.42
  fresnelBoost  0.45
  translucency  0.30
  wetness       0.55
  # THE ALBEDO MOTTLE. surfaceNoiseAmp already roughens the NORMAL, which reads
  # as texture and never as colour, so under this lab's single broad key the
  # creature was one flat tone whichever way it turned. This is the colour
  # twin: fbm in REST space, so the blotches ride a limb through gait instead
  # of swimming over it. Off (0) in every preset, because switching it on
  # changes how a body looks and that is a per-character decision.
  mottleAmp     0.65
  # Low frequency on purpose — patches a hand-span across, not freckles. This
  # is NOT cycles per metre: the shader's fbm multiplies its own input by 4 and
  # 9 for its two octaves, so the number here is about a quarter of the
  # frequency it looks like. The first pass used 5 and produced fine speckle
  # that read as image noise; by ~10 it aliases outright.
  mottleScale   1.6
  # A browner, darker green than the base. Mottling toward something far from
  # the base colour reads as dirt ON the creature rather than variation IN it.
  mottleColor   0.21 0.19 0.06
